      "text": "Testing a browser game requires a comprehensive approach that goes beyond simple visual inspection, as modern web games rely heavily on complex interactions between JavaScript logic, the Document Object Model (DOM), and browser rendering engines. To ensure a robust and enjoyable user experience, developers must systematically validate several critical systems, starting with keyboard input. Testing input involves verifying that every key press triggers the intended action without latency or \"key sticking.\" Developers should test for simultaneous key presses, such as moving left while jumping, to ensure the game handles multiple inputs correctly. Additionally, it is crucial to check for browser-specific behaviors, such as the default scrolling behavior of arrow keys, which must be prevented using `event.preventDefault()` to maintain gameplay fluidity.\n\nCollision detection is another vital area that requires precise testing. Developers must verify that hitboxes align accurately with the visual sprites, ensuring that collisions register only when objects visually touch. This involves testing edge cases, such as fast-moving objects that might tunnel through thin barriers due to frame rate fluctuations. It is also important to test different collision types, such as player-to-enemy, player-to-item, and boundary collisions, to ensure the game logic responds appropriately to each interaction.\n\nScoring systems must be validated for accuracy and persistence. Testers should confirm that points are awarded"
